# spadeConfig.cmake -- generated from this file by configure_package_config_file
# (engine/CMakeLists.txt, next to the existing install(EXPORT spadeTargets
# ...)). This is what makes `find_package(spade CONFIG REQUIRED)` work for an
# out-of-tree consumer at all: install(EXPORT) only emits spadeTargets.cmake --
# the raw target definitions -- and CMake's config mode looks for a file named
# exactly spade<Version>Config.cmake / spadeConfig.cmake, not for an export file
# by name. tests/consumer/ is this repo's own out-of-tree consumer, kept
# in-tree only to build+run against a scratch `cmake --install` prefix.
# .github/workflows/spade.yml, which used to do that, was deleted 2026-09-18;
# scripts/test.ps1 is this repo's gate now.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE GLM GAP, AND WHY THIS FILE'S JOB IS A DIAGNOSTIC, NOT MAGIC.
#
# spade::core's public headers (core/math_ops.hpp) expose glm::vec3/quat/mat3 in
# their own signatures, and every module above it (world/state/physics/sim/
# vehicles) carries that dependency transitively -- a consumer including ANY
# installed Spade header needs glm's headers on its include path. Spade's own
# build gets glm by FetchContent-vendoring it (vendor/CMakeLists.txt,
# pinned to tag 1.0.1), but every link to it in engine/CMakeLists.txt is wrapped
# in $<BUILD_INTERFACE:...> (see spade_core's glm-link comment there for the
# full reasoning): install(EXPORT) refuses an export set whose members require a
# target that is not itself installed/exported, and glm -- header-only,
# vendored, never installed -- is exactly such a target. The practical
# consequence: spadeTargets.cmake does NOT reference glm::glm at all. CMake will
# happily find_package(spade) and define every spade::* target with glm nowhere
# in sight, and the first a consumer would otherwise hear about the gap is a raw
# "glm/vec3.hpp: No such file or directory" from their OWN compiler, pointing at
# nothing in Spade.
#
# This file closes that gap with an explicit, actionable check instead of
# leaving it to be discovered that way. It first tries find_package(glm) itself,
# quietly -- glm may not even be find_package-able on a bare runner (it ships no
# glmConfig.cmake outside a packaging setup), so this is EXPECTED to often do
# nothing, and that is fine, it is a courtesy attempt, not the mechanism this
# file depends on. If glm::glm still does not exist afterwards, find_package(spade)
# fails loudly with instructions instead of quietly succeeding and leaving the
# consumer to debug a missing-header error with no context back to Spade at all.
#
# IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT MAGIC: this file does not fetch or vendor glm on the
# consumer's behalf -- that would mean two copies of glm (this one and whatever
# the consumer's own build already manages) with no guarantee they agree.
# tests/consumer/CMakeLists.txt is the worked example of the obligation
# this message describes: it FetchContents the SAME pinned tag Spade vendors
# (vendor/CMakeLists.txt's GIT_TAG 1.0.1) before its own
# find_package(spade) call.
#
# ONE THING THIS FILE USED TO LEAVE UNCLOSED, NOW FIXED (S6 hygiene): the
# paragraphs above describe DETECTION only, but glm::glm's usage in every
# installed Spade header is header-only, exactly like yaml-cpp's usage below
# is a linked archive -- so once the check above passes, the SAME closure
# mechanism the yaml-cpp section documents at length applies here too, and
# does: this file wires the now-confirmed-present glm::glm onto spade::core's
# link interface (see "THE ACTUAL CLOSURE" below the yaml-cpp check), so a
# consumer who fetched or found glm still only writes ONE
# target_link_libraries(their_target PUBLIC spade::sim) and gets glm's
# include directories transitively, rather than also having to know to link
# glm::glm onto their own target by hand. This does not change anything
# above: the file still never fetches or vendors glm for the consumer, it
# only propagates a dependency that already, provably, exists.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE YAML-CPP GAP -- SAME SHAPE AS GLM'S ABOVE, DIFFERENT CONSEQUENCE, AND A
# DIFFERENT CLOSING MECHANISM (integration follow-up to S5 Task 5's world-file
# reader/writer, surfaced by Task 5's opus review and adjudicated here).
#
# world/world_file.hpp's world_to_yaml()/world_from_yaml()/save_world_file()/
# load_world_file() (S5 Task 5) parse and emit YAML through yaml-cpp, and
# spade_world links it PRIVATE, $<BUILD_INTERFACE:...>-only (engine/
# CMakeLists.txt's spade_world yaml-cpp-link comment) for the identical
# install(EXPORT) reason glm is genex-wrapped: yaml-cpp is a FetchContent
# target (add_subdirectory + add_library, not an IMPORTED find_package
# target) in no export set, and install(EXPORT) rejects any reference to it
# that is not fully hidden behind $<BUILD_INTERFACE:...>/
# $<BUILD_LOCAL_INTERFACE:...> -- VERIFIED EMPIRICALLY that this holds even
# for a same-named entry wrapped in $<INSTALL_INTERFACE:...> (that engine/
# CMakeLists.txt comment records the full finding). That means, unlike a
# textbook external dependency, yaml-cpp::yaml-cpp's presence CANNOT be
# recorded in spadeTargets.cmake's own INTERFACE_LINK_LIBRARIES at all -- this
# file is where the closure actually has to happen instead, see the
# set_property() call below the target checks.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT A COPY OF THE GLM CHECK ABOVE, DESPITE THE SAME SHAPE: glm
# is HEADER-ONLY, so a missing glm::glm is a COMPILE-time gap that hits every
# consumer of any installed Spade header, unconditionally -- gating on it
# unconditionally is simply correct. yaml-cpp is a COMPILED ARCHIVE: a
# consumer that never calls into world_file.hpp never pulls world_file.obj
# out of spade_world.lib (a static archive only extracts the .obj members
# that resolve an otherwise-undefined symbol) and therefore never needs
# yaml-cpp's symbols at all. Gating find_package(spade) unconditionally on
# yaml-cpp::yaml-cpp is therefore STRICTER than every consumer strictly
# needs -- and it is still the right call, deliberately: spadeConfig.cmake
# cannot see, at configure time, whether the CONSUMER's own translation units
# will end up calling world_file.hpp's functions (that is a fact about their
# source, not this file's), so the choice is between (a) checking here,
# unconditionally, with a clear message naming exactly what to do, or
# (b) checking nowhere, and letting a consumer who DOES need it discover the
# gap as a bare CMake generate-time "target yaml-cpp::yaml-cpp was not found"
# error with no context back to Spade -- the same unhelpful failure mode this
# whole file exists to avoid. (a) costs a consumer who never touches world
# files one extra FetchContent block they did not strictly need; (b) costs a
# consumer who DOES touch world files a confusing failure with no path
# forward. That trade is the right one, and it is why this check mirrors the
# glm check's unconditional shape despite the different underlying reason.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

@PACKAGE_INIT@

include(CMakeFindDependencyMacro)

# QUIET, not REQUIRED, and called directly rather than via find_dependency():
# find_dependency() would propagate this config file's own REQUIRED-ness
# (find_package(spade ... REQUIRED)) straight into find_package(glm ...
# REQUIRED), which aborts immediately with CMake's own generic "could not find
# a package configuration file for glm" error the moment glm is not
# find_package-able -- the WRONG diagnostic (it names glm's find_package-ability,
# not Spade's actual obligation) and it fires before the explicit, clearer check
# below ever runs. Calling find_package directly, quietly, and letting it simply
# not define glm::glm on failure is what lets that later check be the one that
# speaks.
find_package(glm QUIET CONFIG)

# Same QUIET-direct-call reasoning as glm above, not find_dependency(): a
# propagated REQUIRED would abort with CMake's generic "could not find a
# package configuration file for yaml-cpp" the moment it's not
# find_package-able, before the explicit check below (which names Spade's
# actual obligation, not yaml-cpp's find_package-ability) ever runs.
find_package(yaml-cpp QUIET CONFIG)

include("${CMAKE_CURRENT_LIST_DIR}/spadeTargets.cmake")

if(NOT TARGET glm::glm)
    message(FATAL_ERROR
"spadeConfig.cmake: glm::glm was not found.\n"
"Every installed Spade header (spade::core and everything linked above it) "
"exposes glm/vec3.hpp, glm/gtc/quaternion.hpp etc. in its own public "
"signatures, but glm is vendored via FetchContent in Spade's own build and is "
"deliberately NOT part of this package's installed export set (glm is "
"header-only and BUILD_INTERFACE-only there -- see "
"Spade's own engine/CMakeLists.txt's spade_core glm-link comment). "
"find_package(spade) cannot provide it for you.\n"
"Fetch the SAME pinned tag Spade itself vendors (Spade's own vendor/CMakeLists.txt) "
"before calling find_package(spade), e.g.:\n"
"    include(FetchContent)\n"
"    FetchContent_Declare(glm GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/g-truc/glm.git GIT_TAG 1.0.1)\n"
"    FetchContent_MakeAvailable(glm)\n"
"or find_package(glm) a compatible install from your own environment instead. "
"Spade's own tests/consumer/CMakeLists.txt is a worked example.")
endif()

if(NOT TARGET yaml-cpp::yaml-cpp)
    message(FATAL_ERROR
"spadeConfig.cmake: yaml-cpp::yaml-cpp was not found.\n"
"spade::world's world_file.hpp API (world_to_yaml()/world_from_yaml()/"
"save_world_file()/load_world_file(), the versioned YAML world-file "
"reader/writer -- S5 Task 5) links yaml-cpp's COMPILED archive, not just "
"its headers -- and like glm above, yaml-cpp is vendored via FetchContent "
"in Spade's own build and is NOT part of this package's installed export "
"set (see Spade's own engine/CMakeLists.txt's spade_world yaml-cpp-link comment). "
"If your program calls into world_file.hpp -- directly, or transitively "
"through anything that does -- your FINAL LINK needs yaml-cpp's symbols and "
"find_package(spade) cannot supply them for you. (If it does not, this "
"check still ran -- see this file's yaml-cpp comment block above for why "
"that is the deliberate, conservative choice.)\n"
"Fetch the SAME pinned tag Spade itself vendors (Spade's own vendor/CMakeLists.txt, "
"GIT_TAG 0.8.0) before calling find_package(spade), e.g.:\n"
"    include(FetchContent)\n"
"    FetchContent_Declare(yaml-cpp GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/jbeder/yaml-cpp.git GIT_TAG 0.8.0)\n"
"    FetchContent_MakeAvailable(yaml-cpp)\n"
"or find_package(yaml-cpp) a compatible install from your own environment "
"instead. Spade's own tests/consumer/CMakeLists.txt is a worked example.")
endif()

# THE GLM CLOSURE -- the yaml-cpp twin below, same mechanism, simpler case.
# spade::core is the IMPORTED target spadeTargets.cmake just defined, exists
# only in the consumer's own CMake run, so this touches nothing Spade's own
# build validates. Unlike yaml-cpp's PRIVATE, conditional-on-actual-symbol-use
# treatment (see that closure's comment for why), glm is a PUBLIC, always-
# needed dependency of spade::core's own public headers, so it is wired
# unconditionally the moment the check above confirms glm::glm exists -- any
# consumer linking spade::core (or anything above it, which is everything)
# transitively gets glm's include directories on its compile line, exactly
# as if spade_core's own $<BUILD_INTERFACE:glm> link (engine/CMakeLists.txt)
# had been installable. tests/consumer/main.cpp is the worked example.
set_property(TARGET spade::core APPEND PROPERTY INTERFACE_LINK_LIBRARIES glm::glm)

# THE ACTUAL CLOSURE: wire the now-confirmed-present yaml-cpp::yaml-cpp onto
# spade::world's link interface, HERE, in the CONSUMER's own CMake run --
# not at spade_world's own target_link_libraries call in engine/CMakeLists.txt,
# where install(EXPORT)'s export-set validation would reject it outright (see
# that file's spade_world yaml-cpp-link comment for the full, empirically
# verified finding: $<INSTALL_INTERFACE:...> does not exempt a same-named
# non-IMPORTED target from that check). spade::world here is the IMPORTED
# target spadeTargets.cmake just defined (include() above) -- a target that
# exists ONLY in the consumer's own CMake run, so appending to its
# INTERFACE_LINK_LIBRARIES touches nothing Spade's own build ever validates.
# The observable effect is exactly what a working install-interface entry
# would have produced: any consumer linking spade::sim (or anything above
# spade::world) transitively pulls yaml-cpp::yaml-cpp onto its final link
# line, which is what actually resolves world_file.obj's yaml-cpp symbols
# when (and only when) something in the link closure calls into
# world/world_file.hpp. See tests/consumer/main.cpp for the worked
# example that calls the loader and proves this closure end to end.
set_property(TARGET spade::world APPEND PROPERTY INTERFACE_LINK_LIBRARIES yaml-cpp::yaml-cpp)

# NO COMPONENTS: spade is ALL-OR-NOTHING BY DESIGN, documented here rather than
# left implicit. find_package(spade) always defines every spade::* target
# (spadeTargets.cmake, included above, is one export set with no per-module
# split), so `find_package(spade CONFIG REQUIRED COMPONENTS world)` is not a
# smaller or faster request than a plain `find_package(spade CONFIG REQUIRED)`
# -- it is the SAME request, and check_required_components() below only ever
# has the empty set to check (this package never calls
# set_package_properties()/set(<pkg>_FIND_COMPONENTS ...) to populate one), so
# it always reports every named component present regardless of whether a
# target by that name actually exists. A consumer that mistypes a component
# name (e.g. COMPONENTS wrold) gets no diagnostic from this mechanism at all
# -- the FIRST real error is CMake's own generic "target spade::wrold not
# found" at the point something tries to link it. If per-module component
# selection is ever wanted (e.g. installing spade::core without spade::sim's
# heavier dependents), it is a real, separate change -- optional COMPONENTS
# in install(EXPORT), per-component find_package() gating in this file, and
# this note updated to describe it -- not a side effect of adding a name here.
check_required_components(spade)
